21:58:32 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: In most online first-person shooters, the game uses a combination of **client-side prediction**, **server-side authority**, and **lag compensation** to make bullet tracking feel instantaneous, even though network latency would otherwise cause noticeable delays. The exact method depends on whether the bullet is a **hitscan** (instant ray) or a **projectile** (a moving object with travel time). Here’s a breakdown of the process: --- ### 1. Two Types of Bullet Mechanics **Hitscan** - When you pull the trigger, the game immediately fires an invisible ray from the center of your screen (or the weapon’s muzzle) forward. - If the ray intersects a player’s hitbox, it’s a hit. There is no travel time – damage is applied instantly from your perspective. - Used in games like *Call of Duty*, *Overwatch* (hitscan heroes), or *Valorant* for most weapons. **Projectile** - The game spawns a physical (simulated) bullet or rocket that moves through the world with a set speed, affected by gravity and drag. - It has a position updated every frame, and collisions are checked continuously. - Used for things like *Battlefield* sniper bullets, *Fortnite* snipers, or rocket launchers in many titles. --- ### 2. The Client‑Server Model (Authoritative Server) Modern online FPS games almost always use a **client‑server** model: - Your game (the client) sends your input (aim direction, fire command, timestamp) to a dedicated server. - The server is the **authority** that decides what actually happens – it validates hits, applies damage, and tells all clients the outcome. - The server runs its own simulation of the game world, independent of any one player’s machine. --- ### 3. How Hits are Detected in Real Time #### **A. On your screen (Client‑Side Prediction)** To hide the delay between your click and the server’s response (typically 20–100 ms), your client immediately simulates the shot: - **Hitscan:** The client performs a local raycast from your camera. If it hits an enemy, it shows instant visual feedback (blood, sparks, hit marker). - **Projectile:** The client spawns and animates the projectile locally, moving it according to physics. It may also predict the first impact point to give you immediate audio/visual cues. This is pure prediction – the server hasn’t confirmed anything yet. Your game then sends a “fire” message, including your exact aim direction and a **timestamp** of when you clicked. #### **B. Server‑Side Validation and Lag Compensation** When the server receives your “fire” message, it knows the message is from the past (because of network travel time). To be fair, the server rewinds the entire game state to the **exact moment you fired**. - It uses the timestamp you sent. - All player positions, hitboxes, and animations are rolled back to that tick. - Then the server performs **its own raycast** (hitscan) or simulates the full projectile flight. This technique is often called **backwards reconciliation** or **lag compensation**. It means if you had an enemy in your crosshair on your screen, the server will see that enemy in the same spot at the moment of the shot – even though the enemy may have moved on the server by the time the packet arrived. For projectiles, the server will spawn the bullet at the rewinded position and simulate its flight forward in time, checking collisions each frame against the ongoing (real‑time) hitboxes of players and obstacles. This can be more complex, but the goal is the same: make sure that from your perspective, a well‑aimed shot always registers. #### **C. Broadcasting the Result** - If the server confirms a hit, it applies damage and sends a “hit confirmed” message to all relevant clients. - Your client then updates the kill feed, health bars, etc., and plays the final feedback (e.g., elimination sound). - If the server decides you **missed** (e.g., because of prediction errors, packet loss, or the enemy was already behind cover due to lag), it sends a correction. Your client might briefly show a blood splatter before it disappears – a common artifact of imperfect synchronization. --- ### 4. Projectile Tracking Across the Network For projectile weapons, extra care is needed to keep the bullet in sync between many players: - **On the shooter’s client:** The bullet is predicted and drawn immediately. Position updates are shared with the server (and sometimes with other clients via peer-to-peer relay). - **On other players’ clients:** They don’t simulate the bullet from your point of view. Instead, the server broadcasts the bullet’s **creation event** (position, velocity, timestamp), and their games can reconstruct the trajectory using **interpolation**. This avoids visual jitter and uses the server’s authoritative path. - **Continuous Collision Detection (CCD):** Fast‑moving projectiles might tunnel through thin objects between frames. The server (and often the client) uses swept collision tests (e.g., a sphere swept from the bullet’s previous to current position) to detect impacts reliably. --- ### 5. Keeping Everything Smooth: Interpolation and Extrapolation - **Interpolation:** When you see other players, you aren’t seeing their exact live position. Your client holds a small buffer (e.g., 2–3 server updates) and smoothly draws their movement between the last two known states. This catches up with reality slightly delayed but looks fluid. - **Extrapolation:** If an update is late (packet loss), the client guesses movement forward for a few frames until new data arrives. This can cause “rubber‑banding” when corrected. For bullets, this means where you **perceive** an enemy standing might be a few milliseconds behind the server. Lag compensation bridges that gap for hitscan, while projectile collisions use the server’s timeline to ensure fairness. --- ### Summary The “real time” feel is an illusion crafted by: 1. **Instant local feedback** (client-side prediction). 2. **Rewinding time on the server** to match what the shooter saw (lag compensation). 3. **Authoritative hit detection** that prevents cheating but respects the shooter’s perspective. 4. **Smooth interpolation** for all non‑shooter players. All of this happens in milliseconds every time you fire, giving the impression that your bullet travels and hits exactly where you aimed, even across the internet.
